course: dictionary
lesson: 3291
title: Finding the main point
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Essentially means basically, in the most important way, when you leave out the small details. It tells the reader what matters most. «Essentially, a bat is a flying mammal.» The small details, like how a bat sleeps, are left out. The main point is that a bat is a mammal that flies.
explain: Writers use essentially to sum up. When you spot it, stop and ask: what is the big idea here? The words after it give the core of the message, so read them slowly.
explain: Essentially can also be a formal word meaning necessarily, something that must be there. You will also meet the phrases essentially the same, is essentially and essentially means.
fact: Essentially means basically, in the most important way.
fact: «Essentially» often comes before the main point of a sentence or a summary.
fact: A good check is to swap in basically: if the sentence still makes sense, you have the right meaning.
fact: In formal writing, essentially can mean necessarily, as something that must be there.
fact: Useful phrases: essentially the same, is essentially, essentially means.
exercise: «Essentially, a bat is a flying mammal.» What does essentially mean here? | Very quickly | Only sometimes | In a small way, with lots of detail | *Basically, in the most important way
exercise: «The two plans are essentially the same.» What does the writer mean? | They are completely different | They are both very long | *Their main points match, even if small details differ | They are the same in every tiny detail
exercise: «Essentially, the story is about a girl who finds a lost dog.» Find and copy the word that signals the main point is coming. | girl | story | finds | *Essentially
test: «Herbal and homeopathic remedies are diluted so much that essentially nothing of the starting substance is left.» What does essentially mean here? | *Basically, almost completely | Carefully, with great care | Happily, with a smile | Loudly, for everyone to hear
test: «Plants essentially need water to live.» In this formal use, what does essentially mean? | They never have water | They might have water, if they like | They have water on sunny days only | *They must have water, because it is necessary
exam: Which meaning fits «essentially» in a summary? | Exactly, to the last detail | Quickly and quietly | *Basically, in the most important way | Once in a while
